\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Two throats at rest: the sign rule (a,b) and the ruler it is measured with (c,d). (a)~Separation change of three $d=12$ pairs one knob apart: the like pair ($\sigma =+1$, heavy dashed) pushes apart, flipping one field ($\sigma =-1$, solid) turns the push into a pull, and the narrow like pair ($a=1$, light dashed) opens $2.8\times $ more slowly---the force follows the throat width, not the mass. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits, sub-cell where the tracker's $0.03$ grid quantum would swamp the early motion. (b)~Pull over push: with $Q\equiv |F_\phi /F_{\rm  grav}|=5$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:binary}), the prediction is $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=3/2$ (burgundy); measured $1.518\pm 0.021$. The gauge-settling systematic (grey band) is common to both arms and cancels in the ratio. (c)~Per-mouth areal radius of freshly placed pairs at $t=0$ (ink circles): wider than the isolated throat ($R_\star $, dotted) by an excess falling as $d^{-1.16}$---the neighbour's field on the ruler. Burgundy squares: the head-on scout's mouths (Sec.~\ref {sec:headon}) at the separation reached; open grey squares (here and in d) lie closer than the closest probe, where no calibration backs the comparison. (d)~The scout against the curve, $\delta R/R=R_{\rm  scout}/R_{\rm  place}-1$: the $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ calibrates the tracker's grid snap, and the throats' own squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$. In the shaded band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound; a boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a real squeeze.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pair}{{3}{7}{Two throats at rest: the sign rule (a,b) and the ruler it is measured with (c,d). (a)~Separation change of three $d=12$ pairs one knob apart: the like pair ($\sigma =+1$, heavy dashed) pushes apart, flipping one field ($\sigma =-1$, solid) turns the push into a pull, and the narrow like pair ($a=1$, light dashed) opens $2.8\times $ more slowly---the force follows the throat width, not the mass. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits, sub-cell where the tracker's $0.03$ grid quantum would swamp the early motion. (b)~Pull over push: with $Q\equiv |F_\phi /F_{\rm grav}|=5$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:binary}), the prediction is $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=3/2$ (burgundy); measured $1.518\pm 0.021$. The gauge-settling systematic (grey band) is common to both arms and cancels in the ratio. (c)~Per-mouth areal radius of freshly placed pairs at $t=0$ (ink circles): wider than the isolated throat ($R_\star $, dotted) by an excess falling as $d^{-1.16}$---the neighbour's field on the ruler. Burgundy squares: the head-on scout's mouths (Sec.~\ref {sec:headon}) at the separation reached; open grey squares (here and in d) lie closer than the closest probe, where no calibration backs the comparison. (d)~The scout against the curve, $\delta R/R=R_{\rm scout}/R_{\rm place}-1$: the $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ calibrates the tracker's grid snap, and the throats' own squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$. In the shaded band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound; a boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a real squeeze}{figure.3}{}}
